  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe upper low over the forecast area will slowly begin to\nmeander to the northeast through the rest of the day and into\ntonight. Expect rain to persist within the proximity of the low\nthrough the rest of the day as well. With some dry pockets, \nsome wet bulbing will allow brief periods of snow mixing in with\nthe precipitation. This will occur more frequently within the \nfoothills and also with temperatures remaining in the 30s \nthrough the rest of the day. As well, probabilities show \ncontinued wind gusts through the day in the 25 mph to 35 mph \nrange through the day at about the 50% to 70% range in the low \nlands for 30 to 35 mph winds. The rest of the stronger winds \nwill remain the PA and WV ridges with 40 to 50 mph wind \npossible. \n\nThe exception is eastern Tucker County where slightly higher\nelevations and stronger upslope favors SLRs more in the 10:1 to\n13:1 range while surface temperatures hover closer to freezing.\nThe result is higher probabilities for accumulations in the 1-3\"\nrange that may stick (even if will be on the wet-side). The\npresence of snow may change heading into Saturday morning as the\nupper low exits, with some guidance suggesting the lose of ice\nnuclei as moisture depths narrows and lends to more freezing\ndrizzle (more likely after midnight tonight). When taking these\nwintry mixes together, add in the prior ice that occurred early\nthis morning and the return of wind gusts in the 40-50 mph range\nas a SW low-level jet comes overhead, localized impacts may \ndevelop so a Winter Weather Advisory remains in effect.\n\nConfidence is high that impacts cease later Saturday morning\nthanks to height rises and dry southwest flow.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe next weather concern will arrive on the heels of the exited\nupper low as it exits the New England region. The track will\ntake the low center through the Mid MS Valley and northeast\nthrough the Upper OH Valley. This will bring some warmer air to\nthe region as well with rainfall onset by Sunday afternoon. At\nthis time, the precip is expected to be rain, at least in the\nlower elevations but by 12Z Monday, a change over to snow in the\nhigher elevations is expected. Current trends are for less than\nan inch and even the NBM is giving a 60% to 70% probability of\nsnow greater than an inch. As well, with another strong 50 knot\n850MB Jet, another round of near wind advisory winds can be\nexpected. This will be a likely potential for the WV and PA\nridges. At this time, its a 90% prob of advisory criteria\nSunday night through Monday night. An NPW may be needed.",
